{
  "_note": "Community newbie tier lists, transcribed verbatim. Two SEPARATE rankings: how well a character carries as main DPS, and how much they improve a team as support. Keeping them apart is the whole point \u2014 Kathigu-Ra is A as a DPS and C as a support, Clementine is the reverse, and the single `tier` field on annotations/awakeners.json cannot express either. `dpsFloor` / `supportFloor` record the investment level the source printed in parentheses next to a portrait, e.g. \"Mouchette (E2)\"; that is the level at which the rank applies, and it is role-specific, so a unit can be listed at different floors on the two lists (Murphy: Fauxborn is E2 as a DPS and unqualified as a support). Absent from a list means the source did not rank them there at all \u2014 per its own note, you would need a crazy reason to use them in that role. That is meaningful data, so absence is left as absence rather than backfilled with a low grade.",
  "_dpsScale": {
    "S": "Insane \u2014 needs very little investment to steamroll story mode and D-Effect Zone.",
    "A": "Great \u2014 clears normal story as main DPS even with a suboptimal team; good in D-Tide Alert 5.",
    "B+": "Good early \u2014 clears normal story as main DPS, but needs specific teammates or high investment for endgame.",
    "B": "Decent \u2014 clears normal story as main DPS, but needs specific teammates or high investment to finish all chapters.",
    "C": "Usable \u2014 an unrealistic amount of effort for a new player to clear story with; better used later as support or secondary DPS."
  },
  "_supportScale": {
    "S": "Insane \u2014 improves almost any team by being on it.",
    "A": "Great \u2014 a premium support, strong in a variety of situations; lets weak teams punch above their weight.",
